<SiteLayout title="UTF-8 BOM in Subtitle Files: What It Is & When to Use It | ConvertAnyFile" description="Learn what a UTF-8 BOM is in subtitle files, when it can help compatibility, how it differs from LF/CRLF line endings, and how to control it when converting subtitles.">
<section class="page-hero"><div class="site-container hero-inner"><div class="breadcrumbs"><a href="/">Home</a><span>/</span><a href="/guides/">Guides</a><span>/</span><span>UTF-8 BOM subtitles</span></div><span class="eyebrow">Subtitle encoding</span><h1>What is a UTF-8 BOM in subtitle files?</h1><p>A UTF-8 BOM is a short byte marker placed at the beginning of a text file. Modern software usually does not require it, but some older subtitle tools and Windows-oriented workflows use it as an encoding hint.</p></div></section>
<ArticleFrame toc={toc} related={related} relatedTitle="Related subtitle tools & guides">
<div class="quick-answer" id="quick-answer"><strong>Quick answer</strong><p>Leave UTF-8 BOM off unless the software that will open the subtitle expects it. If a subtitle displays non-ASCII characters incorrectly in a legacy tool, enabling BOM can sometimes improve encoding detection without changing the subtitle text itself.</p></div>
<h2 id="what">What exactly is a UTF-8 BOM?</h2><p>BOM stands for byte order mark. UTF-8 does not need byte ordering in the same way UTF-16 does, but the three-byte UTF-8 BOM is sometimes used to signal that a text file is encoded as UTF-8. It appears before the first visible character and is normally hidden by applications that understand it.</p><p>For subtitle files, the BOM does not change cue timing, dialogue, or format structure. It only changes the bytes at the beginning of the output file.</p>
<h2 id="when">When can a BOM help?</h2><ul><li>An older subtitle editor guesses the wrong character encoding.</li><li>Accented, Cyrillic, Japanese, Korean, Chinese, or other non-ASCII text displays incorrectly in a legacy application.</li><li>A downstream workflow explicitly documents that UTF-8 with BOM is required.</li></ul><p>For modern browsers, editors, and media tools, plain UTF-8 without BOM is often the cleaner default.</p>
<h2 id="line-endings">UTF-8 BOM is not the same as LF or CRLF</h2><p>These settings solve different problems. BOM is an encoding marker at the beginning of the file. LF and CRLF describe how line breaks are stored throughout the text. A file can be UTF-8 with BOM and LF, UTF-8 without BOM and CRLF, or another valid combination.</p><div class="callout"><strong>Choose for the destination</strong><p>Do not enable BOM just because it sounds safer. Use the simplest output that the destination accepts, and only add compatibility markers when the receiving software needs them.</p></div>
<h2 id="steps">How to control BOM when converting subtitles</h2><ol><li>Open the <a href="/subtitle-converter/">Subtitle Converter</a>.</li><li>Add the subtitle file and let preflight detect the source encoding.</li><li>Choose the output format.</li><li>Enable <strong>UTF-8 BOM</strong> only when required by the destination.</li><li>Choose LF or CRLF separately if the receiving workflow has a line-ending requirement.</li><li>Convert and test the file in the target software.</li></ol>
<h2 id="faq">Common UTF-8 BOM questions</h2><h3>Does BOM change subtitle content?</h3><p>No. It adds an encoding marker before the text.</p><h3>Is BOM required for UTF-8?</h3><p>No. UTF-8 works without it. BOM is mainly a compatibility hint for certain software.</p><h3>Can BOM fix broken characters?</h3><p>Sometimes, if the receiving application is misdetecting UTF-8. It cannot repair text that was already decoded or saved incorrectly.</p><h3>Should every SRT use BOM?</h3><p>No. Only use it when the destination benefits from it.</p>
